					<item>
				<guid>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-399147#post-1394761</guid>
				<title>(no title)</title>
				<link>http://www.scpwiki.com/forum/t-399147/scp-897#post-1394761</link>
				<description></description>
				<pubDate>Wed, 14 Mar 2012 19:20:40 +0000</pubDate>
				<wikidot:authorName>Pineapple of Despair</wikidot:authorName>				<wikidot:authorUserId>554825</wikidot:authorUserId>				<content:encoded>
					<![CDATA[
						 <p>This, or more specifically the same principle applied to the drowning described in the article, is exactly what bugs me about this otherwise highly enjoyable article. The guy is drowning in a river. His lungs fill with water and get swapped with the tourist's. However, he is quite likely still drowning in a river! What are the chances the few gulps of air he's got with the new lungs allow him to recover? Not great, methinks. In other words, one doesn't need lava to trigger a chain reaction involving 897&#8230; Hell, if one applied the locked-box criterion here this SCP could even be classified as Keter. And yet, it seems no Foundation researcher or agent familiar with it has found it worth even a casual mention in the report&#8230; Odd.</p> 
				 	]]>
				</content:encoded>							</item>
